Title:
polyshell — Polyglot Reverse Shell Dropper Generator

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- Polyshell is a reverse-shell “dropper” generator that outputs a self-contained `sh` script designed to run on a compromised *nix target.  
- The generated script iterates through multiple common interpreters/binaries and attempts to connect back, stopping at the first successful payload.  
- It targets reliability in heterogeneous environments (servers, containers, embedded/BusyBox) where you don’t know what tooling is installed.  
- The repo also provides ready-to-use injection one-liners (e.g., `curl|sh`, `wget|sh`) and simple encoding options (base64/URL) to help with basic filtering/WAF constraints.  
- Useful primarily for red teamers and pentesters during RCE/command-injection/SSRF-to-RCE workflows where fast, adaptable reverse shells matter.  
- Interesting because it standardizes a “try-everything” reverse shell chain and includes practical nuances (PTY via Python, nc variant handling, BusyBox coverage).

Technical Focus:
- Polyglot reverse shell chaining via interpreter discovery (`command -v`)
- Shell dropper generation (`curl|sh` / `wget|sh` execution patterns)
- Payload catalog and extensibility (`payloads.py`, custom chain blocks)
- Encoding/evasion helpers (base64, URL encoding, decimal IP)
- Netcat/socat/ncat compatibility handling and mkfifo fallback
- PTY stabilization via Python `pty.spawn`

Use Cases:
- Rapid reverse shell establishment after command injection/RCE
- “Unknown environment” post-exploitation where installed interpreters vary
- Generating one-liners for web exploit chains (RCE, SSRF pivot to command exec)
- Basic filter/WAF bypass attempts using encoding and whitespace tricks (`$IFS`)
- Customizing payload sets for specific target fleets (containers/embedded)

Keywords:
reverse shell, dropper, polyglot, curl|sh, wget|sh, command injection, RCE, SSRF, bash, python3, python2, perl, php, ruby, socat, netcat, ncat, busybox, mkfifo, pty.spawn, WAF bypass, base64, URL encoding, $IFS, decimal IP